\alias{LearnerRegrStochtree}
\title{Stochastic Tree Ensemble BART Regression Learner}
\description{
Bayesian additive regression trees (BART) sampled with MCMC,
optionally warm-started with the grow-from-root algorithm of accelerated BART (XBART).
Calls \code{stochtree::bart()} from \CRANpkg{stochtree}.

The \code{"se"} predict type returns the posterior standard deviation of the conditional mean.
Leaf regression bases and random effects are not supported.
}
\section{Custom mlr3 parameters}{

\itemize{
\item \code{stochtree::bart()} takes most of its parameters in the lists \code{general_params}, \code{mean_forest_params},
and \code{variance_forest_params}.
The entries of these lists are exposed as individual hyperparameters.
The entries of \code{general_params} and \code{mean_forest_params} keep their names.
The entries of \code{variance_forest_params} whose names clash with those of the mean forest are prefixed with
\code{variance_}, i.e., \code{variance_num_trees}, \code{variance_alpha}, \code{variance_beta}, \code{variance_min_samples_leaf},
\code{variance_max_depth}, \code{variance_keep_vars}, \code{variance_drop_vars}, and \code{variance_num_features_subsample}.
\item \code{random_seed}: If not set, the seed of the C++ random number generator is drawn from R's random number generator,
so that results are reproducible with \code{set.seed()}.
Upstream seeds from \code{std::random_device} in this case.
\item \code{observation_weights} is not exposed as a hyperparameter.
Observation weights are taken from the task instead, see the \code{weights} property.
}
}

\section{Saving a Learner}{

The fitted model holds pointers to C++ objects.
In order to save a trained learner, or to use it with encapsulation or parallelization,
the model is marshaled to a JSON string with \code{stochtree::saveBARTModelToJsonString()}.
Call \verb{$marshal()} before saving the learner with \code{saveRDS()} and \verb{$unmarshal()} after reading it.
}

